<p>
  <strong>The substitution distance says nothing here.</strong> The entry counts a deep rung as
  a multi-step crossing where d, the distance from the world's dominant metabolism tape when
  the last lower rung reached the line to the first deep solver after it, is at least 2. All 9
  read d ≥ 2, from 17 to 31, so all 9 are "multi-step" by the locked rule. The rule cannot tell
  here. The worlds hold 9 634 to 14 375 distinct metabolism tapes among 16 384 cells, and the
  dominant one sits on only 9 to 21 cells. Drift puts it a median 22 to 30 of 32 bytes from a
  cell of its own world. So the test could only fail where the dominant tape is the deep solver
  itself. Read with the study's own tool instead, every mutant within 3 substitutions of the
  dominant tape (not locked):
</p>
<ul>
  <li>no child has a credited path within 3, one that gains a paid unit at every step;</li>
  <li>the nearest deep mutant is 2 substitutions away in 4845 and 4871, 3 in 4863, 4952 and
    4980, and beyond 3 in 4862 and 4961;</li>
  <li>in 4890 and 4979 the dominant tape was already an EQU solver at the line, below a tenth
    of the world, so no rung was crossed between the two tapes the distance compares.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  <strong>Heritability: 0 heritable, 8 unresolved, 1 not heritable (4979).</strong> Each first
  deep solver was planted back into its own end world, every other carrier of the rung wiped,
  beside a control with the same wipe and no planting. In 14 of the 15 locked plantings, every
  seed's control re-grew the deep rung to a tenth of the world within 500 epochs, level with the
  planted world, which the entry reads as unresolved. In 4979 neither world reached a tenth. In
  10 of the 16 plantings, a labelled extension for 4890's XOR included, the tape the wipe put
  in place, the world's commonest tape not credited the rung, is one substitution from it. So in these worlds the deep rung is a property of the
  population: re-derived within hundreds of epochs from abundant lower-rung tapes one
  substitution below it, not a single lineage's invention. That is what the design study's
  §7.6 describes: "the soup then evolves loop-shaped ORN/ANDN/OR solvers that sit one
  substitution below XOR/EQU, and takes that step".
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The circuits.</strong> Every deep solver is a loop that stacks NANDs leftward, lap
  after lap, and reads earlier laps' results. Each is credited its rung on all 18 traced cases
  and fans out an input. They compute XOR in 4 to 6 NANDs, but for 4863's, a 12-NAND chain ending in a double
  negation, and EQU in 5 to 12.
</p>

<h2>What it means</h2>
<p>
  Given a tape apart from the copier, read by a NAND that keeps its operands, and paid for every
  rung, a soup derived from emerged BFF worlds assembled XOR and EQU in a sixth of its worlds.
  Paid for those two alone it built nothing deep, and with the NAND that writes in place it
  built nothing deep either. That is Lenski et al.'s 2003 stepping-stone mechanism, complex
  features from rewarded simpler ones, in a population that began as a soup. It also turns the
  design study's pilot reading of the Logic stall into a measurement: with the copier out of
  the path, the in-place NAND still built no deep rung (H-deep-M refuted), and the stack NAND
  did (H-stack shown).
</p>
<p>
  It holds on a machine built for it. The substrate imports four things: <strong>an
  objective</strong>, the paid tasks; <strong>a primitive</strong>, the NAND byte;
  <strong>a hereditary channel</strong>, since the world, not the program, copies the
  metabolism tape; and <strong>the primitive's semantics</strong>, the stack NAND, chosen among
  seven because it was the one under which the pilot's deep rungs came.
</p>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Nothing about rung 4 on Soup.</strong> Whatever rose here was paid for by tasks the
    programme chose, computed by a byte and a tape the programme added. It is never pooled
    with the fitness-free runs, and rung 4 on Soup stays "not shown".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not open-ended.</strong> The ladder tops out at EQU. A child that holds it has
    nowhere further to be paid for.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Depth is what the loop's later laps compute.</strong> As the
    <%= link_to "topless study", "#{studies_url}/topless.md" %> puts it (§2.3–2.4), it is "the
    circuit complexity of the function the soup's lap structure happens to compute", a real
    computation, not a count of accumulated steps.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not a lineage's invention.</strong> No deep rung read heritable, and the
    substitution distance does not separate a crossing from drift in these worlds.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Two parents, under one re-reading.</strong> The deep tests hold whichever one or two
    parents are left out of all 18. Without the piloted parents they read 8 to 0, and that
    re-reading rests on parents 967 and 2771, which gave 4 of the 9 deep children.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Children are correlated.</strong> Three children share each parent's world, four
    parents were piloted, and two arms are the Logic sweep's own children.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What comes next</h2>
<p>
  The ladder here has a near top. The <%= link_to "topless study", "#{studies_url}/topless.md" %>
  asks whether depth keeps rising when it has none: nested three- and four-input logic ladders,
  each rung paid by its exact minimal NAND count, read for whether the deepest rung held keeps
  rising late in long runs. Its minimal-NAND tables are computed; the engine slice that adds the
  ladders is in review. A pre-registration of its own comes after.
</p>
